content.text:
Changing `theme.sidebarCollapsedWidth` on its own won't widen the strip you actually see. You also need to stop hardcoding the strip width in `Sidebar.tsx` and keep the icon from being faded out. Here's what that value currently controls:

| What | Where | Uses `sidebarCollapsedWidth`? |
|---|---|---|
| Page content margin when collapsed | `app/components/Layout.tsx:107` | ✅ yes |
| Temporary width while dragging the sidebar closed | `Sidebar.tsx:80` | ✅ yes |
| **How much of the collapsed sidebar stays on screen** | `Sidebar.tsx:420` (`calc(-100% + 16px)`, or 8px on the desktop app) and the RTL rule (`8px`) | ❌ **hardcoded** |

If you only change the theme value, the page content moves over but the visible strip stays 16px (8px in the desktop app and in RTL). You'd get an empty gap between the strip and the content.

## 2) Make the collapsed strip wider

**`shared/styles/theme.ts:49`**
```ts
sidebarCollapsedWidth: 32, // was 16; fits a 20px icon with padding
```

**`Sidebar.tsx`, in the `breakpoint("tablet")` block**: base the transform on the theme value:
```ts
transform: translateX(${(props: ContainerProps) =>
  props.$collapsed
    ? `calc(-100% + ${props.theme.sidebarCollapsedWidth}px)`
    : 0});

[dir="rtl"] & {
  transform: translateX(${(props: ContainerProps) =>
    props.$collapsed ? `calc(100% - ${props.theme.sidebarCollapsedWidth}px)` : 0});
}
```
This removes the `Desktop.hasInsetTitlebar() ? 8 : 16` special case. The narrower 8px strip in the desktop app is too thin for an icon, so all three cases should use the same width now. A wider strip also makes the hover area that reveals the sidebar a bit bigger, which suits this feature.

## 1) Add the icon to the collapsed strip

Put the icon inside `Container` and pin it to the inner edge (`inset-inline-end: 0`). Because it moves with the sidebar, it sits exactly in the visible strip.

The dimming problem is the `& > div { opacity: 0 }` rule. It only matches direct `div` children, so making the icon wrapper a `span` gets around it without editing that rule. Don't reuse `ToggleButton` either, because its default style is `opacity: 0`.

```tsx
import { SidebarIcon } from "outline-icons";
```

In the JSX, next to `<ResizeBorder>`:
```tsx
{canCollapse && (
  <CollapsedHint aria-hidden $visible={collapsed && !isHovering}>
    <SidebarIcon size={20} />
  </CollapsedHint>
)}
```

The styled component:
```ts
const CollapsedHint = styled.span<{ $visible: boolean }>`
  display: none;
  position: absolute;
  top: ${Desktop.hasInsetTitlebar() ? 40 : 0}px; // same offset as SidebarButton's top padding
  inset-inline-end: 0;
  width: ${(props) => props.theme.sidebarCollapsedWidth}px;
  height: 48px;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  color: ${s("textTertiary")};
  pointer-events: none;
  opacity: ${(props) => (props.$visible ? 1 : 0)};
  transition: opacity 150ms ease-in-out;

  [dir="rtl"] & svg {
    transform: scaleX(-1);
  }

  ${breakpoint("tablet")`
    display: flex;
  `}
`;
```

Why it's set up this way:
- **It's always rendered and fades using `$visible`** (instead of `{collapsed && …}`). The icon fades in as the sidebar collapses and fades out when a hover slides the sidebar open. Without the fade, it would appear at the far edge of the expanded sidebar.
- **`pointer-events: none`** lets mouse movement over the icon reach the `Container`'s `onPointerMove`, so hovering the icon still reveals the sidebar.
- **It's hidden below the tablet breakpoint**, because the sidebar doesn't collapse on mobile.
- **It sits at the top, 48px tall**, which roughly lines up with the header row where the real toggle button is. Check this visually and adjust if it's off.
- **`aria-hidden`**: the icon is only a visual hint, and the real toggle button already has the "Expand sidebar" label.

Optionally, you could make the icon clickable to expand the sidebar permanently: remove `pointer-events: none`, turn the wrapper into a button that calls `ui.toggleCollapsedSidebar()`, and add an `aria-label` plus a new key in `translation.json`. As written above, no new translation strings are needed.

To test it, collapse the sidebar with the toggle button, with Ctrl+., and by dragging it closed. Check each in LTR and RTL, and in the desktop app if you use it (the inset-titlebar offset).

Want me to make these changes on `feat/issue-28-toggle-sidebar-icon`?

